  "summary": "Aesthetic quality of out-of-focus areas in photographs, valued for its circular light points and smooth rendering.",
  "description": "Characterized by soft, circular highlights in blurred backgrounds, created by lens aperture shape and design; valued in portrait and product photography for its pleasing aesthetic quality. Developed as photography technology advanced and lens manufacturers began optimizing for this specific quality.",
  "culturalContext": "Bokeh quality — the aesthetic of out-of-focus areas, from Japanese boke (blur) — turned lens character into a cult: Helios 44-2 \"swirly\" bokeh, Canon's nifty-fifty circles, Petzval vintage swirls, anamorphic oval highlights. Lens collectors and cinematographers chase rendering as identity (the \"glow\" of Cooke look), apps simulate it, and \"creamy bokeh\" is the lingua franca of portrait-photography desire — blur as luxury texture.",
